    {
      "id": "00a749da-995d-4012-8ab4-787b5f330987",
      "phaseId": "36628b50-a0b5-4974-852e-96d68c09f11f",
      "number": 90,
      "shortId": "VJ7K5",
      "priority": 4,
      "shortName": "build-nficon",
      "title": "Build NfIcon widget with NfGlyph enum",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "Widget icon con enum di glyph semanticamente nominati.\n\n## Partially landed early, in F003/P085/T358 (2026-07-30)\n\nThe command palette's keycaps needed key glyphs, and neither existing face could give them: Phosphor has **no keyboard glyphs at all**, and the UI face (Geist Mono) has no `⌃ ⌥ ⌘ ⎋ ⇞ ⇟`. So the core of this task was built there:\n\n- **`FontRole::NerdFont`** (`heca-grid-ui/src/scene.rs`) — a third role beside `Text`/`Icon`. The renderer maps it to `font::NERD_FONT_FAMILY` in `heca-renderer/src/scene.rs`, riding the per-run family override the terminal path already had, so `text.rs` needed no new plumbing.\n- **`NfGlyph` + `NfIcon`** (`heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/nf_icon.rs`) — single-layer (a Nerd Font glyph is one codepoint, not a duotone pair), same color-inheritance contract as `Icon`.\n- **`PaintCx::nf_icon`**, and `paint_keycap_nf` / `KeyCap::Nf` so a keycap can draw one.\n- **Showcase gallery** (`NfGlyph::ALL` with name + codepoint) and a `docs/widgets.md` § NfIcon.\n\n**No second font was embedded** (see T098): the app already ships **Maple Mono Normal NF** as the terminal face — a real Nerd Font, loaded unconditionally at renderer init and addressed by family name, so a user's own terminal font cannot take the glyphs away from the UI.\n\n### What is left for this task\n\n`NfGlyph` currently covers **the keyboard set only** — Shift, Control, Option, Command, CapsLock, Enter, Escape, Tab, Space, Backspace, ArrowUp/Down/Left/Right. T103 (wire NfIcon into the program catalog) needs the **program/devicon** glyphs, which are not in the enum yet. Adding one = variant + `codepoint()` + `name()` + `ALL`.\n\n### The trap for whoever adds glyphs\n\n`heca-renderer/tests/font_coverage.rs` proves every codepoint is **in** the embedded font (a missing one would render as tofu, which no other test can see). It **cannot** prove a name matches its picture: the font names its glyphs `uniF0636`. The five `nf-md-apple_keyboard_*` codepoints were read off the Material block's alphabetical order (caps, command, control, option, shift = F0632..F0636) and **must be confirmed by eye in the showcase gallery** — same caveat `Glyph::CaretLeft` carries. If one is off by one it is a one-character fix.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
